---
import DocsLayout from "@/layouts/DocsLayout.astro"
---

<DocsLayout
  title="Laravel Sail (Docker)"
  path="/dev/laravel-sail"
  section="dev"
  description="Run KoAkademy locally with Laravel Sail's per-project Docker environment."
>
  <p>
    Sail is a lightweight per-project Docker environment for Laravel: PHP,
    Composer, Node, and the services you choose (PostgreSQL, Redis, and more)
    all run in containers controlled with <code>vendor/bin/sail</code>. On
    Windows, everything runs through WSL2.
  </p>

  <h2>1. Install Sail</h2>
  <p>From the KoAkademy checkout, add Sail and choose its services:</p>
  <pre><code>composer require laravel/sail --dev
php artisan sail:install --with=pgsql,redis</code></pre>
  <p>
    The <code>--with</code> option selects PostgreSQL and Redis non-interactively;
    running <code>php artisan sail:install</code> without it shows the service menu.
    This publishes a <code>docker-compose.yml</code> in the project root and a PHP
    image folder (for example <code>docker/8.5</code>) that the compose file
    references.
  </p>

  <h2>2. Configure .env</h2>
  <p>
    Point KoAkademy at Sail's containers. These values match Sail's default
    container credentials:
  </p>
  <pre><code>DB_CONNECTION=pgsql
DB_HOST=pgsql
DB_PORT=5432
DB_DATABASE=koakademy
DB_USERNAME=sail
DB_PASSWORD=password

REDIS_HOST=redis
CACHE_STORE=redis
SESSION_DRIVER=redis
QUEUE_CONNECTION=redis</code></pre>
  <p>
    <code>DB_HOST=pgsql</code> is the compose service name, exactly like
    <code>postgres</code> in the production topology.
  </p>

  <h2>3. Start and migrate</h2>
  <pre><code>./vendor/bin/sail up -d
./vendor/bin/sail artisan key:generate
./vendor/bin/sail artisan migrate</code></pre>
  <p>
    The first <code>sail up</code> builds the image and takes a few minutes. Sail
    serves the application on port 80, so open
    <code>http://localhost/setup</code> to create development data and the first administrator.
  </p>

  <h2>4. Frontend work</h2>
  <pre><code>./vendor/bin/sail npm ci
./vendor/bin/sail npm run dev</code></pre>
  <p>or build once with <code>./vendor/bin/sail npm run build</code>.</p>

  <h2>5. Gotenberg (optional)</h2>
  <p>
    Sail has no Gotenberg service by default. If you work on PDF generation, add
    it to the published <code>docker-compose.yml</code>:
  </p>
  <pre><code>  gotenberg:
    image: gotenberg/gotenberg:8
    restart: unless-stopped
    ports:
      - '3000:3000'</code></pre>
  <p>
    then set <code>GOTENBERG_URL=http://gotenberg:3000</code> in <code
      >.env</code
    >
    and run <code>./vendor/bin/sail up -d</code> again.
  </p>

  <h2>6. Validation and daily commands</h2>
  <pre><code>./vendor/bin/sail composer test
./vendor/bin/sail artisan test --compact
./vendor/bin/sail down</code></pre>
  <p>
    Add a shell alias so <code>sail</code> works from the project root:
  </p>
  <pre><code>alias sail='[ -f sail ] &amp;&amp; sh sail || sh vendor/bin/sail'</code></pre>

  <div class="doc-callout doc-callout-note">
    <p class="doc-callout-label">Windows and WSL2</p>
    <p>
      On Windows, install Docker Desktop with the WSL2 backend and run every
      command inside a WSL2 distribution. Keep the project on the WSL filesystem
      — not <code>/mnt/c</code> — for acceptable file I/O performance.
    </p>
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
